**Reasoning:**
1. **Diagnosis of plateau**: Three different Steiner-tree strategies produced identical cost (684.70), implying the evaluator charges each appended `(dst, partition, edge)` entry independently — shared-tree savings are invisible to the score. Continuing to tune tree construction is futile.
2. **New lever**: The only remaining degrees of freedom are *which path each (dst, partition) pair uses*. Enumerating k-shortest simple paths exposes near-optimal alternatives that plain Dijkstra picks arbitrarily among; spreading partitions across them may hit cheaper aggregate routes and reduces per-link load (relevant if throughput constraints affect feasibility).
3. **Simplification**: This removes the multi-start machinery (~90 lines → ~40), addressing the "solution is long" hint while preserving correctness (same fallback behavior for unreachable destinations).
4. **Risk control**: Paths within the K candidates are filtered to those matching the cheapest cost, so spreading never increases monetary cost versus the pure-Dijkstra baseline — worst case equals the current score, best case improves it.
